{
  "requirement": "A provider failure that ends the model conversation returns\n`ValidationError::Agent` to the awaiting ask builder.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation. When a provider failure ends the model conversation, the awaiting ask builder must get `ValidationError::Agent`.\n\n- **Main test:** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#fn[assistant_text_and_provider_failure_cannot_become_typed_results]` is marked `{test}` for the requirement. Its second half builds a `ToolAgent` over `Script::new(vec![None])`. In `impl[Script/CompletionProvider]/fn[complete]`, a `None` turn returns `io::Error::other(\"permanent provider failure\")`. The test awaits `ctx.ask::<bool>()` and asserts it is `Err(ValidationError::Agent(error))` with a message containing \"permanent provider failure\". That checks both the variant and that the provider's failure reaches the caller. It also asserts that no dependencies were recorded.\n- **Second test:** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[configured_agents_initialize_only_when_asked_and_report_setup_errors]` is also marked. A configured agent with a missing credential fails the awaited `verify`. The test asserts `ValidationError::Agent` and that the message names the missing credential variable. This is closer to a setup failure than a conversation failure, so it only adds supporting coverage.\n- **Possible gap:** the scripted failure happens on the first turn. The requirement does not separate first-turn failures from mid-conversation ones, so I did not treat a mid-conversation failure as a distinct obligation.\n\nNo gap was found, and there are no acknowledgments.",
  "references": "0fbc2bd5fac34b56faf1a32f5f18e07e5f7165136fc32734f4466834b4898eda",
